Cloning a GitHub repository involves creating a local copy of a remote repository on your computer. This allows you to work with the code, make changes, and then push those changes back to the remote repository.
Steps to clone a GitHub repository:
Navigate to the repository on GitHub: Open the repository's page in your web browser.
Locate the "Code" button: On the repository's main page, above the list of files, find and click the green "Code" button.
Choose a cloning method and copy the URL:
HTTPS (Recommended for most users): Under the "HTTPS" tab, click the clipboard icon to copy the HTTPS URL. This method typically requires you to authenticate with your GitHub username and password when performing operations like pushing changes.
SSH (For users with SSH keys configured): If you have an SSH key set up and added to your GitHub account, select the "SSH" tab and copy the SSH URL. This method uses your SSH key for authentication.
GitHub CLI (If you have GitHub CLI installed): If you prefer using the GitHub command-line interface, select the "GitHub CLI" tab and copy the provided command.
Open your terminal or Git Bash: Launch your preferred command-line interface (e.g., Terminal on macOS/Linux, Git Bash on Windows).
Navigate to your desired directory: Use the cd command to change your current working directory to where you want to store the cloned repository. For example:
Code

    cd /path/to/your/projects
Clone the repository: Use the git clone command followed by the copied repository URL.
For HTTPS or SSH:
Code

        git clone <repository-url>
Replace <repository-url> with the URL you copied in step 3. For GitHub CLI.
Code

        gh repo clone <owner/repository-name>
Replace <owner/repository-name> with the appropriate values (e.g., octocat/Spoon-Knife).
Press Enter: The git clone command will execute, downloading the repository files to a new directory in your current location, named after the repository.
You now have a local clone of the GitHub repository, ready for development.
